steps.body:
`java.util.Arrays`, the class you already use for `toString`, has static helpers for arrays:

```java harness
import java.util.*;

public class Main {
    public static void main(String[] args) throws Exception {
        int[] a = {42, 7, 19, 3, 25};
        int[] b = {-1, -1, -1, -1, -1};
        int[][] gridA = {{1, 2}, {3, 4}};
        int[][] gridB = {{1, 2}, {3, 4}};
        {{code}}
        System.out.println(Arrays.toString(a));
        System.out.println(Arrays.equals(a, b));
    }
}
```

```java
Arrays.sort(a);                    // ascending, in place
Arrays.fill(a, -1);                // every slot becomes -1
Arrays.copyOfRange(a, 1, 4);       // new array of a[1], a[2], a[3]
Arrays.equals(a, b);               // same length and same values
Arrays.deepEquals(gridA, gridB);   // the same check for 2D arrays
Arrays.binarySearch(a, 19);        // index of 19 in a sorted array
```

`sort` changes the array itself, because the method gets a copy of the reference, just like `doubleAll` in the Methods lesson. Sort a `clone()` when you still need the original order. There is no descending sort for `int[]`: sort ascending, then loop from the last index down. `copyOfRange` excludes its end index, like `substring`.

`binarySearch` only works on a sorted array. A missing value gives a negative result, `-(insertionPoint) - 1`, so test `index >= 0`. With duplicates it may find any one of them.

`==` compares references, and `Arrays.equals` on a 2D array only compares row references. Use `deepEquals` for grids.

`Arrays.asList(names)` views a `String[]` as a fixed-size `List`, handy for `contains`. Calling `add` or `remove` on it throws. It is a varargs method, so `Arrays.asList("Ana", "Ben")` works too. Careful: its varargs type must be an object type, so `Arrays.asList(new int[]{1, 2})` treats the whole `int[]` as one value and gives a `List<int[]>` of size 1.

example.code:
import java.util.Arrays;

public class Main {
    public static void main(String[] args) {
        int[] nums = {42, 7, 19, 3, 25};
        int[] sorted = nums.clone();
        Arrays.sort(sorted);
        System.out.println("Original: " + Arrays.toString(nums));
        System.out.println("Sorted: " + Arrays.toString(sorted));

        System.out.print("Largest first:");
        for (int i = sorted.length - 1; i >= 0; i--) {
            System.out.print(" " + sorted[i]);
        }
        System.out.println();

        int[] marks = new int[4];
        Arrays.fill(marks, -1);
        System.out.println("Filled: " + Arrays.toString(marks));
        int[] middle = Arrays.copyOfRange(sorted, 1, 4);
        System.out.println("Middle: " + Arrays.toString(middle));

        int[] other = {3, 7, 19, 25, 42};
        System.out.println("Equal: " + Arrays.equals(sorted, other));
        System.out.println("Same array: " + (sorted == other));
        int[][] gridA = {{1, 2}, {3, 4}};
        int[][] gridB = {{1, 2}, {3, 4}};
        System.out.println("Grids equal: " + Arrays.deepEquals(gridA, gridB));

        System.out.println("Index of 19: " + Arrays.binarySearch(sorted, 19));
        System.out.println("Index of 20: " + Arrays.binarySearch(sorted, 20));

        String[] names = {"Ana", "Ben", "Cy"};
        System.out.println("Has Ben: " + Arrays.asList(names).contains("Ben"));
    }
}

