        System.out.println("Index of 90: " + missing);

        String[] names = {"Lena", "Omar", "Priya"};
        boolean enrolled = Arrays.asList(names).contains("Omar");
        System.out.println("Omar enrolled: " + enrolled);
    }
}

steps.body:
`java.util.Collections` does for lists what `Arrays` does for arrays. Add `import java.util.Collections;` at the top.

```java harness
import java.util.*;

public class Main {
    public static void main(String[] args) throws Exception {
        List<String> names = new ArrayList<>(List.of("Mia", "Ana", "Leo"));
        List<Integer> ages = new ArrayList<>(List.of(30, 25, 41, 30));
        {{code}}
        System.out.println(names);
        System.out.println(ages);
    }
}
```

```java
Collections.sort(names);                             // ascending, in place
Collections.sort(ages, Collections.reverseOrder());  // descending
Collections.reverse(names);                          // flip the order
Collections.swap(names, 0, 1);                       // swap two indexes
Collections.max(ages);                               // largest element
Collections.min(ages);                               // smallest element
Collections.frequency(ages, 30);                     // how many equal 30
Collections.binarySearch(names, "Mia");              // index in a sorted list
```

They order `String` values by `compareTo`, so upper case letters come before lower case, and wrappers by value. `sort`, `reverse`, and `swap` change the list in place, so it must be mutable. A `List.of(...)` list throws, so copy it first with `new ArrayList<>(List.of(...))`. `frequency` compares with `equals`, so it counts `Integer` values correctly even above 127.

`binarySearch` works like `Arrays.binarySearch`: the list must be sorted ascending (not with `reverseOrder()`), and a missing value gives a negative result. `max` and `min` throw on an empty list, so check `isEmpty()` first. `Collections.shuffle` exists too, but it gives a new order on every run.

example.code:
import java.util.ArrayList;
import java.util.Collections;
import java.util.List;

public class Main {
    public static void main(String[] args) {
        List<String> names = new ArrayList<>(List.of("Mia", "Leo", "Zoe", "Ava", "Leo"));
        Collections.sort(names);
        System.out.println("Sorted: " + names);
        System.out.println("Mia at: " + Collections.binarySearch(names, "Mia"));
        System.out.println("Bob at: " + Collections.binarySearch(names, "Bob"));

        Collections.sort(names, Collections.reverseOrder());
        System.out.println("Descending: " + names);
        Collections.swap(names, 0, 1);
        System.out.println("Swapped: " + names);
        Collections.reverse(names);
        System.out.println("Reversed: " + names);

        System.out.println("Max: " + Collections.max(names));
        System.out.println("Min: " + Collections.min(names));
        System.out.println("Leo count: " + Collections.frequency(names, "Leo"));
    }
}

